Somewhere around 2005, an Indian developer in Hong Kong went through a small philosophical quest, intrigued by the mystery of oriental China and imperial Japan. It was an interesting time for Hong Kong; the British had already left for their shores, China had not fully arrived, and it was magical. Highly energised crowd with a passion for the latest gadgets, fashionable clothes, lots of outdoor eating, weekend hobbies, lots of exciting nearby destinations and good people willing to help with wonderful staying localities made it an excellent place for young guys like us who are exploring career choices in far-off lands. After all, these South Asian countries are the largest and oldest magnificent civilisations where thousands of philosophers have walked the path and guided us to create meaning out of life from a mere existence.

Article content
Park Island, Hong Kong
Somehow, I landed in Park Island after moving around quite a few places: a service apartment in Wan Chai, an apartment shared with a wonderful Sindhi family, to a remote apartment at the edge of Hong Kong and finally made this beautiful island my home for the next couple of years along with few colleagues who became very good friends along the way. Along with a few assignments of enhancing the software platform, I was responsible for designing a resource manager to effectively manage and plan the movement of containers, different cranes, like yard and shore cranes, to optimise cost. I also did good work in the ingestion of manifest files for ship movement and taking care of customs check, truck entry, gate handling, etc., using EDI through a capable open-source software named OpenAdaptor, which was using a state engine and capabilities for different formats of input and output handling, transformation etc.

Article content
DP World Terminal
The presence of another fellow Indian developer who was hell bored simply due to his more capable nature with a scientific mindset flared the interest even more. He shyly confessed that he was secretly reading Chinese philosophy like The Art of War by Sun Tzu, a highly revered military general from the 5th BC in the Eastern Zhou dynasty. By now, the readers must have figured out that it's me and my dear colleague in the little quest. In alignment with our spirited youth, we took the current organisational setup and tried to see what we can apply from Chinese philosophy to the existing set of people, projects and behaviours. Over copious amounts of tea and coffee, we had long discussions while walking in the large and long corridor of a 13th-floor massive building inside one of Hong Kong’s busy terminals overlooking ships and containers lined up in the yard with massive cranes loading containers into giant trucks. We are pretty sure others were living in parallel universes, even to make sense of what we are doing, let alone care about what we have to say. And what did we find out? Humans are still the same after thousands of years, but that is the topic of another day.

Article content
Starbucks, Kwai Fong
Somehow I didn't like the premise of The Art of War, as in modern life, it is not more about how vicious you can be to win, as those types of victory can be short-lived; rather, it is about how to act in your highest form of integrity so that you can build trust and attract like-minded people to form your tribe. One fine Saturday morning, while my roommates are deciding between squash and swim, I decided to dash to a bookstore in Times Square, but not before devouring a whole mushroom black pepper Quiche and washing it with a nice cup of steaming hot dark roasted Ethiopian coffee from Starbucks. I lunged directly to the philosophy section of the book shop and started scanning the books like airport security.

Article content
Bookshop, Causeway Bay
Suddenly, I saw one title out of a hundred books poking out of the bookshelf. The cover gave it away as it is a Samurai in his full, glorious combat wear. The Book of Five Rings, authored by the 16th-century legendary Japanese Samurai Miyamoto Musashi, who had never lost a duel. To my adventurous self, it meant something, in medieval Japan 16th century, which was one of the most unsettling and disturbing periods. How could a Samurai remain unbeatable and live to tell the tale? A little reminder is that during that period, losing a duel means losing your life or losing your reputation, which is worse than death in a society with a high honour code, not a mean feat, I thought. What an unbelievable guy, but what shocked me is that he credited all his wins to his mind and not his body. What? I picked up the little book and settled into one of the reading stools in a corner. I will leave the interesting details of that book for readers to explore if they are interested, but one thing stands out from his philosophies - the middle way. It is Miyamoto Musashi's interpretation of a balanced, non-extreme approach to life and combat, aligning with broader philosophical principles like Buddhism's Middle Way. I picked the book, got it billed and started for home in the private ferry of Park Island.

