Article content
Sonthra Kebab, Wan Chai
In 2025, again slowly, a space started emerging where it seems that this middle path can be exercised with its full glory, GenAI. After all, where can you find such extremes in the form of an AI that is taking the world by storm? Billions of dollars, great minds, AI laws, chip wars and customised chips, data wars, infra scaling, plethora of AI tools, hordes of people jumping to the AI bandwagon like it’s a gold rush, new features by AI providers almost every day, highly polarised opinions; so how can you look away, you human. I have my healthy scepticism toward GenAI in its place and am fairly confident that I can use my critical thinking to contain it to its rightful place whenever required.

After reading lots of research papers on GenAI and LLM architecture, studying its mathematical foundation, hearing the views of many researchers, practitioners and entrepreneurs and playing around with AI tools, I decided to build a view about GenAI in code generation similar to the middle way, i.e., not to follow the extremes. There is a huge leadership push as well, with colleagues and customers jumping on it and discussing it daily. So GenAI has won the boardroom, it seems, as it plays the theatrics very well. On one side, there is a view that GenAI is overhyped to lead to AGI, and at another extreme, GenAI is said to replace jobs. I decided to park my critiques aside and explore it. So, how shall I think about GenAI/LLMs? Can Musashi’s middle path guide again for AI exploration, neither left nor right, but centre. Why not put on your builder hat, think of GenAI as a good tool and create something with it to build your opinion? This is to check what the tool is really capable of, and where I place it in my existing toolset. It is somewhere between the hype and zero-utility; what is its actual place?

I was playing with GitHub Copilot for some time and creating varieties of application. Recently, in a daly long workshop I got an Amazon Q subscription from AWS partners, which is a GenAI capable of code generation supported by Claude Sonnet 4, arguably the best LLM for code generation out there. We tried an extensive end-to-end SDLC spec driven development based approach to create a project of medium complexity and were surprised by the result. It could create User stories, high-level design, low-level design, Code implementation and even identify Design patterns while gluing them together, all from carefully crafted intelligent prompts. It could also add the elements of security, performance considerations, format compliances even multithreading as a choice of implementation. It gave me a choice of languages, tech stack, database, middleware, messaging, caching, etc. Not perfect, but a good start. I thought of trying something in a shorter format.

I decided to use Java and later regretted it a bit due to the verbosity it created. The regret was more due to my self-imposed time constraint, as for LLM's it doesn't matter. You can always generate the implementation code in a different language, which I think is a big advantage. I am somewhat impressed despite the limitations. It's not a silver bullet, not a genie either, but it's a solid tool in the hands of an expert.

I got reasonably excited, which felt like I could keep on going with this thing forever, so one colleague light-heartedly commented that it is like doom scrolling! After I was back home, wifey commented that you have a huge smile of happiness today in your face and are looking happy like you used to, what hit you?

The first Sunday after the event, I picked up a mini goal, i.e., to build an ESG(Environmental, Social, and Governance) dashboard in a small footprint, sans the full phased SDLC-based implementation, only the front end, back end and dummy data. The ESG scoring framework evaluates a company’s performance across Environmental, Social, and Governance dimensions, providing investors and stakeholders with a holistic view of its sustainability practices and risk profile. A quick and good enough prototype that can be demonstrated. I decided to follow a minimal approach of Prompt->Code generation->Debugging->Deployment, skipping the extensive SDLC phases.

This time, I used Python for both front-end and backend. From the provided prompt, it generated the implementation code, tried to run it, co-debugged multiple times and voila, finally deployed. I looked under the hood, and it generated multiple files by using modularity. The code is neatly written, well formatted, and can act as a good scaffolding for more ambitious projects. I tried updating the frontend by moving component positions, adding more test data, and adding new UI components, all with prompts, and it worked with little or no interference. It ran the server inside Visual Code and created a small instruction manual on how to access it. It created a Dashboard which looked like this after a few tweaks.

Article content
ESG Dashboard
What blew my mind is that the whole thing took just a couple of hours on a lazy Sunday morning! So, the highly intelligent and directionless intern has arrived; you need to guide them.

Some observations - GenAI/LLM can be used to create quick and effective deployable and runnable prototypes. With the right context engineering, you can also create smaller pieces of components and then glue them together, which can be effective. As we can think much faster than we can code, this can be advantageous to create the initial scaffolding. As of now, it will struggle when you try to extend it to more complex applications. Code generation is swift, but bigger change sets and debugging are challenging.

Catch - Creating a production-grade application of medium/high complexity is a different project altogether and needs broader and wider skillsets supplemented by decades of software engineering experience supported by well grounded principles. You need architecture, planning, collaborations, security, performance, and quality considerations, which hold good for any decent-sized software project. Code creation is only a small part of the larger equation. So here we are.

I have shared the code in the GitHub repository - https://github.com/satyajitmails/ESG-Dashboard. Please go through and provide comments if any.

Will AI ever take my job, like ever? Frankly, dear, I don’t give a damn! Enjoy the ride and see where it leads you.

